hypotheses.statement:
The group $\Gamma$ is finitely generated and has property (T). The map $\theta$ is injective, $$[\Gamma:\Lambda]=8,
 \qquad t\gamma t^{-1}=\theta(\gamma)\quad(\gamma\in\Gamma).$$ There is a nonzero finite-dimensional irreducible unitary representation $\pi_*$ of $\Gamma$ that factors through reduction modulo $2$ and is nontrivial on the translation subgroup $\mathbb Z^3$. In particular, $$\label{eq:group-aperiodic}
 \pi_*\not\simeq\pi_*\circ\theta^j\qquad(j\geq1).$$

outputs.exact_statement:
The group $\Gamma$ is finitely generated and has property (T). The map $\theta$ is injective, $$[\Gamma:\Lambda]=8,
 \qquad t\gamma t^{-1}=\theta(\gamma)\quad(\gamma\in\Gamma).$$ There is a nonzero finite-dimensional irreducible unitary representation $\pi_*$ of $\Gamma$ that factors through reduction modulo $2$ and is nontrivial on the translation subgroup $\mathbb Z^3$. In particular, $$\label{eq:group-aperiodic}
 \pi_*\not\simeq\pi_*\circ\theta^j\qquad(j\geq1).$$

Let $w=(w_j)_{j\in\mathbb Z}$ be positive and decreasing. Suppose that $\log w_j$ is convex for $j\le0$ and concave for $j\ge0$, that $$\label{eq:root-sum}
 \sum_{j\ne0}w_j^{1/j}<\infty,$$ and that, for some $a>\log3$, $$\label{eq:quadratic}
 \lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{\log w_n}{n^2}=-a,
 \qquad
 \lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{\log w_{-n}}{n^2}=a.$$ Then the closed $R$-invariant subspaces of $\ell^2(w)$ are precisely $$\{z:z_j=0\text{ for }j<h\},\qquad h\in\mathbb Z\cup\{-\infty,+\infty\}.$$ Consequently the shift $Te_j=(w_{j+1}/w_j)e_{j+1}$ on $K$ has invariant lattice $\{K_{\ge h}:h\in\mathbb Z\cup\{-\infty,+\infty\}\}$.

For every $x\in X_*$ there is a finite $C(x)$ such that, for all $n,q\ge1$, $$\begin{aligned}
 \sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\Delta a_k(x)&\ge Dn-C(x),\label{eq:positive-drift}\\
 \sum_{k=-q}^{-1}\Delta a_k(x)&\le-Dq+C(x).\label{eq:negative-drift}
\end{aligned}$$

For $x\in X_*$, the coefficients $\widetilde b_j(x)$ tend to zero as $j\to+\infty$ and as $j\to-\infty$. Consequently there are measurable functions $b_j:X\to[0,1]$ which are positive on $X_*$ and satisfy $$\label{eq:commuting-coefficients}
 b_{j+1}(x)\beta_j(x)=b_j(x)\beta_{j-1}(\theta x)
 \qquad(x\in X,\ j\in\mathbb Z).$$
